('629', 'mbe', 'mbe_0629', 'prompt_0943', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '74', '', 'What majority of Congress is necessary to overturn a presidential veto?', 'Two-thirds of either house.', 'Two-thirds of the Senate.', 'Two-thirds of the House of Rep- resentatives.', 'Two-thirds of both houses.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('630', 'mbe', 'mbe_0630', 'prompt_0944', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '75', '', 'Assume for this question only, that the President challenges Congress'' power to enact this act. Which of the following propositions are correct? I. Federal judicial selection is solely a presidential decision. II. The Act is unconstitu- tional.', 'I and II.', 'I only. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 179', 'II only. Questions 76 — 77 are based on the following facts:', 'None of the above. During a senate debate, U.S. Senator Tom Semand argues that the scientific work pro- duced by XYZ University "is not up to the standards of grammar school — let alone fit for a $150,000 federal grant".', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('631', 'mbe', 'mbe_0631', 'prompt_0945', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '76', '', 'If XYZ University sues Semand for defamation, his best defense will be:', 'First Amendment Freedom of Speech.', 'Article I Speech and Debate Clause.', 'XYZ was a public entity.', 'Sovereign immunity.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('632', 'mbe', 'mbe_0632', 'prompt_0946', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '77', '', 'Assume for this question only, that it can be proven that Semand received a $20,000 bribe from ABC University, the competitor of XYZ University, for the $150,000 grant. May Semand be sued for taking a bribe in connection with the federal grant application?', 'No, since there was sovereign immunity.', 'No, because this kind of thing is difficult to prove.', 'No, because of the Speech and Debate Clause.', 'None of the above. 180 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 0 = 0 Questions 78 — 79 are based on the following facts: Governor Pilson, governor of the fictional state of Columbia, is indicted in Columbia for the embezzlement of state funds. United States President George, convinced that such is a paltry political attempt to destroy one of his closest allies, pardons Pilson for "any and all crimes committed while in office, up to and including those committed as of this date."', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('633', 'mbe', 'mbe_0633', 'prompt_0947', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '78', '', 'If the Columbia Attorney General chal- lenges this pardon, she will:', 'Fail, because such is one of the enumerated presidential pow- ers.', 'Fail, because the President''s power is limited to pardon for political crimes.', 'Succeed, because the Presi- dent''s power is limited to federal crimes.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('634', 'mbe', 'mbe_0634', 'prompt_0948', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '80', '', 'The ambassador to the United States from the fictional foreign republic of Sheisterdam, while touring the United States, gets drunk while driving in the state of Oregon and is arrested by the Oregon police. In fact, the ambassador was not legally drunk and was unaware of the Oregon prohibition of driving while intoxicated. The local district attorney decides to prosecute. What is the ambassador''s best defense?', 'Yes, if the crimes were commit- ted while Pilson was in office. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 181', 'He was not drunk.', 'He was ignorant of the law.', 'Oregon does not have jurisdic- tion over foreigners in their state.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('635', 'mbe', 'mbe_0635', 'prompt_0949', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '83', '', 'What is the state''s best argument why Eleventh Amendment. their prohibition should be upheld?', 'There is no constitutional right Tenth Amendment. to engage in consensual homo- sexuality.', 'There is no constitutional right', 'Be dismissed, because of the', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('636', 'mbe', 'mbe_0636', 'prompt_0950', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '84', '', 'What is the defendants'' best argu- ment?', 'There is a constitutional right to', 'There was a Due Process Clause violation.', 'The state rule served no rational basis. 182 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'The state had no compelling interest.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('637', 'mbe', 'mbe_0637', 'prompt_0951', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '85', '', 'Under existing U.S. Supreme Court precedent, who is likely to succeed on federal appeal?', 'I and II only.', 'I only.', 'III only.', 'None of the above, because this is an open issue.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('638', 'mbe', 'mbe_0638', 'prompt_0952', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '87', '', 'Seeme Clothiers, of Lemon County, challenges the outright ban. Will they succeed?', 'No, because such is part of the state''s police powers.', 'No, because of the Privileges and Immunities Clause.', 'No, because the President is the Commander-in-Chief.', 'Yes, because of the Tenth Amend- ment.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('639', 'mbe', 'mbe_0639', 'prompt_0953', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '89', '', 'Assume for this question only, that Lemon County has also banned all legal and medical advertising — because they are "unprofessional and have decreased the quality of services." How should these regulations be analyzed if chal- lenged? Questions 87 — 89 are based on the', 'Vagueness Doctrine.', 'Ripeness Doctrine.', 'Commercial Speech Doctrine.', 'Just Compensation Clause.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('640', 'mbe', 'mbe_0640', 'prompt_0954', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '90', '', 'The fictional state of Barrier requires all of its attorneys to pass a moral fit- ness exam in order to be licensed. The burden is on the applicant to show good moral character. Applicant Spendthrift, 10 years earlier, had declared bank- ruptcy. In the last 8 years, however, he has established a fine credit rating and has held a steady job. Spendthrift, after passing the bar, is denied a law license by the Barrier Supreme Court, due to his prior bankruptcy. What will be Spendthrift''s best argument on federal appeal?', 'He is now morally fit.', 'Significant time has passed.', 'Barrier''s ruling is contrary to Congress''s intent to allow bank- rupt persons to start over.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('641', 'mbe', 'mbe_0641', 'prompt_0955', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '92', '', 'If Junior is summarily suspended for one month from Jalonia Junior High School, his constitutional rights have been: II. Yes, if he was punished because it was burned in an unreasonable time, place and manner. III.No, because there was no hearing. IV. No, because there is a right to burn the U.S. flag in political protest.', 'Denied, because he was not given a prior hearing.', 'Denied, because flag-burning is a right.', 'Not denied, because it was not a specific political protest.', 'Not denied, because it was like shouting "Fire!" in a crowded theatre.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('642', 'mbe', 'mbe_0642', 'prompt_0956', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '94', '', 'Assume for this question only, that Junior''s parents take him out of the Jalonia Junior High School, and enroll him in the Bush Academy, a private school. Junior again burns a U.S. flag. What is Junior''s best argument in ap- pealing this suspension?', 'Procedural due process.', 'Substantive due process.', 'First Amendment.', 'None of the above. I. Yes, if he was punished because it was not his flag. 186 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('643', 'mbe', 'mbe_0643', 'prompt_0957', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '95', '', 'Intent on restoring family values, the state of Alabama has just enacted a statute whereby illegitimate children cannot sue in tort for the wrongful death of their parents. Is this a lawful state regulation?', 'Yes, because of the Tenth Amend- ment.', 'Yes, because Alabama has a permissible interest in so legis- lating.', 'Yes, because Alabama has a ra- tional basis in so legislating.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('644', 'mbe', 'mbe_0644', 'prompt_0958', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '97', '', 'The state of Hawaii legislature, still angered at the gross inequities placed on the many by the few old landown- ers before the state made that conduct illegal, pronounces a list of the twenty worst offenders. It also fines them $25,000 each and declares them guilty of a "misdemeanor against the People of the State of Hawaii." They are not sentenced, however, to any time in jail. Under which of the following consti- tutional clauses will these individuals have a successful claim? I. Just Compensation. II. Fifth Amendment Due Process. III.The Bills of Attainder Clause. IV.The Ex Post Facto Clause.', 'I, II, III and IV.', 'III and IV only.', 'II only.', 'None of the above, since no jail time was imposed. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 187', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('645', 'mbe', 'mbe_0645', 'prompt_0959', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '98', '', 'Fictional President George, after a highly difficult, but successful, re-elec- tion campaign, feeling duly grateful to his Chief of Staff Snaker, bestows upon him the title of "Royal Knight." The title has no significance whatsoever, but there is an official ceremony at the White House. Ned Rader challenges this gesture in federal court. Will Rader succeed?', 'No, because the title has no sig- nificance.', 'Yes, because it is a title of Nobil- ity.', 'No, because it is analogous to a Presidential Medal.', 'No, because Rader has no stand- ing. Questions 99 — 100 are based on the following facts: Rawson is sentenced to death at the capital- sentencing phase of his murder prosecution. Over objection of the defense, the jury is told that Rawson is a member of a white supremacist group. Rawson is sentenced to death and appeals the conviction based on the prosecutor''s allusion to his membership in the white supremacist group.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('646', 'mbe', 'mbe_0646', 'prompt_0960', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '99', '', 'What will Rawson''s best constitutional argument be?', 'That he is being subjected to Cruel and Unusual Punishment.', 'That his Freedom of Association Rights were abridged.', 'That his Free Speech rights were abridged.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('647', 'mbe', 'mbe_0647', 'prompt_0961', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '100', '', 'If Rawson''s white supremacist group did not advocate disobedience to the law, and if both Rawson and the victim are white, will Rawson prevail?', 'No, because Victim Impact state- ments are admissible.', 'No, because the error was harm- less.', 'No, because it was relevant.', 'Yes, because it was irrelevant. 188 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('648', 'mbe', 'mbe_0648', 'prompt_0962', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '101', '', 'A story is written about movie star Birdie Jones, in the tabloid, the Nation- al Perspirer. In the story, it is revealed that Birdie is infected with genital herpes. If Jones sues the newspaper in tort for defamation, she will:', 'Prevail, because such is damag- ing.', 'Prevail, if the story was know- ingly false.', 'Prevail, since she has a right to privacy.', 'Fail, because she does not have a right to privacy. ND OF QUESTIONS CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 189 190 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW CONSTITUTIONAL LAW ANSWERS A is the correct answer. Junior''s claim is one of the Establishment of Religion in violation of First Amendment. B is in- correct because it is not a Speech claim. It is true that First Amendment claims against a state entity are made applica- ble against the state by the incorporation of some of the Bill of Rights under the Fourteenth Amendment. However, t', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('649', 'mbe', 'mbe_0649', 'prompt_0963', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '1', '', 'Mick is on a rough corner waiting for a bus. Officer Stone sees Mick and since he has nothing else to do, decides to investigate Mick. Stone walks up to Mick and says: "Hold it right there, mister." Mick takes flight and throws away a marijuana joint which was on his person. Mick is prosecuted for illegal marijuana possession. Mick''s attorney files a motion to suppress the marijuana. This motion will: I. Fail, because Mick has no standing. II. Fail, because Mick aban- doned the property. III. Fail, because Mick was not "seized."', 'I only.', 'II and III only.', 'I, II and III.', 'None of the above. Questions 2 — 3 are based on the following facts: Marie is arrested on a charge of solicita- tion to commit prostitution. Her arrest is performed by an undercover officer. She is brought to jail. Three days later, still in jail, she appears before a judge who refuses to let her free on bail. Marie files a writ to the state supreme court, demanding release.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('650', 'mbe', 'mbe_0650', 'prompt_0964', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '3', '', 'Assuming that the state supreme court orders Marie be set free, she may have a civil claim against the arresting of- ficer.', 'True, provided he was in bad faith.', 'True, regardless of his bad faith.', 'False.', 'None of the above. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 233 Q', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('651', 'mbe', 'mbe_0651', 'prompt_0965', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '4', '', 'Since illegal drugs are often transported by transients taking Blackhound buses, Officer Grey decides to investigate the Blackhound station in his precinct. He obtains consent of the local bus station manager to enter buses and request if luggage can be searched. Grey enters a bus at 11:00 p.m., before it is to leave, then announces the following to the seven people in the bus: "Hi, my name is Jack Grey. May I search each of your bags?" One by one, Grey walks to each passenger seat and asks for their bag. Passenger Dawn hands over her purse, which has cocaine in it. At trial for Dawn''s cocaine possession, is the cocaine admissible against Dawn?', 'No, because there was no warrant to search.', 'No, because there was no exi- gency.', 'Yes, because of the automobile exception.', 'Yes, because of the consent ex- ception. Questions 5 — 7 are based on the following facts: Acid is driving his car at 70 m.p.h. in a 55 m.p.h. zone. He is pulled over by Officer Quist. Quist asks Acid if he can search the passenger compartment of Acid''s car. Acid says, "Yes." Quist finds a closed bag in the back seat and in it is LSD (contraband). Acid says, "I didn''t mean to allow you to get into that!" Quist then opens Acid''s trunk and finds a closed suitcase. Quist opens it and finds $10,000', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('652', 'mbe', 'mbe_0652', 'prompt_0966', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '5', '', 'When Quist pulled Acid over, there was a:', 'Detention.', 'De facto arrest.', 'Illegal detention.', 'Legal traffic stop.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('653', 'mbe', 'mbe_0653', 'prompt_0967', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '6', '', 'Quist''s opening of the closed bag in the passenger compartment was:', 'Beyond the scope of the consent given.', 'Within the scope of the consent given.', 'A lawful search incident to ar- rest.', 'A lawful search under the drug exception to the warrant require- ment. 234 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q - 0 `1 73Om PIE oz c > 73 mi-', 'A', '', '', 0, '')
